1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,670
You were alive for about three years...
that you have zero memory of.

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:04,880
You laughed.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:05,680
You cried.

4
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:09,350
You learned to walk, to talk,
to recognize your own name.

5
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:11,800
Someone fed you every single day.

6
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:13,440
You had a favorite toy.

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:18,220
You had entire relationships — with people who
loved you more than anything in the world.

8
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:19,480
And it's all gone.

9
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:20,630
Not blurry.

10
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:21,570
Not faded.

11
00:00:21,570 --> 00:00:22,330
Gone.

12
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:26,830
Three years of your own life, and
you cannot produce a single frame.

13
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:33,590
So here's the question almost nobody asks: if you
can't remember any of it... who was that baby?

14
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:35,160
Try it right now.

15
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:36,940
What is your earliest memory?

16
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:38,330
Go ahead.

17
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:39,410
I'll wait.

18
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:48,750
Whatever you just thought of — a birthday, a beach, falling off something, a dog
that scared you — odds are you were about three and a half years old.

19
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:52,290
That's the average, across almost every
study ever done on this.

20
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:55,740
Almost nobody remembers anything
from before age two.

21
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:57,940
And between two and four?

22
00:00:57,940 --> 00:00:59,100
Just fragments.

23
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:02,030
Random flashes with no story attached.

24
00:01:02,030 --> 00:01:03,480
A patch of carpet.

25
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:04,390
A smell.

26
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:07,170
The feeling of being carried
somewhere in the dark.

27
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:12,010
Scientists have a name
for this: infantile amnesia.

28
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:15,260
And here is why it is genuinely strange.

29
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:18,900
Those first years are the most
important of your entire life.

30
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:22,270
You learned more, and faster,
than you ever will again.

31
00:01:22,270 --> 00:01:25,070
An entire language, from nothing.

32
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:27,560
How to operate a human body.

33
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,080
Who your family is.

34
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,490
Whether the world is safe or hostile.

35
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:37,550
Your brain recorded the most valuable data it will
ever collect...
...and then apparently threw it in the trash.

36
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:39,030
Not some of it.

37
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:39,970
All of it.

38
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:44,410
So why would evolution build a
brain that deletes its own childhood?

39
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:47,410
Your first guess is
probably the obvious one.

40
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:49,910
Babies just can't form memories yet.

41
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:51,490
Their brains aren't finished.

42
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:54,640
Nothing got recorded, so
there's nothing to remember.

43
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,140
Nice theory.

44
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:57,300
It's wrong.

45
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:05,170
In the nineteen eighties, a psychologist named Carolyn Rovee-Collier ran
an experiment that is equal parts adorable and mind-blowing.

46
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:09,600
She tied a ribbon from a baby's ankle
to a colorful mobile hanging over the crib.

47
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:12,680
The baby kicks — the mobile spins.

48
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:16,300
Babies work this out within
minutes, and they love it.

49
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,620
Kick, spin, delight.

50
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:20,540
Kick, spin, delight.

51
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,160
Here's the twist.

52
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:25,630
Bring that same baby back
days or even weeks later.

53
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:29,810
Same crib, same mobile —
but this time, no ribbon.

54
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:32,440
The baby immediately starts kicking.

55
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,870
It remembered.

56
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:38,380
And not just the mobile — it
remembered what it had learned to do.

57
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:41,860
Two-month-olds held onto that memory
for a couple of days.

58
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:45,140
Six-month-olds, for around two weeks.

59
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,160
Babies form memories perfectly well.

60
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:53,700
They remember faces, places, routines,
whole sequences of events.

61
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:55,820
So the recordings existed.

62
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:57,240
Your brain made them.

63
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,500
You were in there, paying attention.

64
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:02,450
Which makes the real
question so much creepier.

65
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:03,730
Where did they go?

66
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:05,730
Meet your hippocampus.

67
00:03:05,730 --> 00:03:14,840
It's a small, curled structure buried deep in your brain, and it is the
part that files away memories of events — what scientists call episodic memory.

68
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:16,330
Your first kiss.

69
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:17,650
Your worst haircut.

70
00:03:17,650 --> 00:03:21,630
That thing you said in twenty fourteen that still
wakes you up at three in the morning.

71
00:03:21,630 --> 00:03:23,050
All filed in there.

72
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:26,300
And in a baby, the
hippocampus has a problem.

73
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,340
It is still under construction.

74
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:35,400
It's one of the only regions of the brain that keeps
producing brand-new neurons at full speed — a process called neurogenesis.

75
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,720
In a baby, it runs at absolute maximum.

76
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,710
Which sounds like good news.

77
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:43,430
New neurons, more learning power.

78
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:47,590
Except in twenty fourteen, a team
of researchers found the catch.

79
00:03:47,590 --> 00:03:54,370
When new neurons wire themselves into the hippocampus, they don't
politely wait their turn at the end of the line.

80
00:03:54,370 --> 00:04:00,900
They barge straight into circuits that already exist —
the exact circuits that are holding your memories.

81
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,300
So the researchers tested
it in both directions.

82
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:10,300
They took adult mice, whose neurogenesis has slowed to a
trickle, and cranked it back up to baby levels.

83
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:14,060
Those adults started forgetting things
they had already learned.

84
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:17,580
Then they slowed neurogenesis
down in infant mice.

85
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:20,000
And the babies remembered longer.

86
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,180
Read that again, because it turns
the whole thing inside out.

87
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:29,600
The very process that was building your brain was shredding
the files as fast as you could save them.

88
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,170
You did not fail to record your babyhood.

89
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:34,090
Your own brain recorded over it.

90
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:35,900
Like a security tape on a loop.

91
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,470
But biology is only half of the answer.

92
00:04:38,470 --> 00:04:44,270
Because there's a second thing missing in a baby,
and honestly it's stranger than the first one.

93
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:46,550
There was no "you" yet.

94
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:49,560
An episodic memory isn't just information.

95
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:54,830
It's information filed under a heading, and
the heading reads: this happened to me.

96
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:56,960
Which requires a me.

97
00:04:56,960 --> 00:05:04,880
A sense of yourself as a specific person who exists across time
— who was here yesterday, and who will still be here tomorrow.

98
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:06,830
Babies don't start with that.

99
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:13,910
There's a famous test for it: you put a dot of red makeup
on a small child's nose, then sit them in front of a mirror.

100
00:05:13,910 --> 00:05:17,500
Before about eighteen months, the
child reaches toward the mirror.

101
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:19,710
They see a baby with a red nose.

102
00:05:19,710 --> 00:05:26,680
Somewhere between eighteen months and two years, something clicks
— and instead they reach for their own nose.

103
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,390
That is the moment a person shows up.

104
00:05:29,390 --> 00:05:33,020
And it lands right at the edge
of where memory starts to stick.

105
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:35,220
Probably not a coincidence.

106
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:37,260
Then there's language.

107
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:39,750
And this is the detail
that really gets me.

108
00:05:39,750 --> 00:05:47,280
Researchers taught toddlers to operate a strange toy machine, at an age
before those children had the words to describe what they were doing.

109
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:52,790
Then they waited months, and came back after
those children had learned to talk properly.

110
00:05:52,790 --> 00:05:55,270
The children could still work the machine.

111
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:57,280
Their hands remembered it perfectly.

112
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:03,530
But when they were asked to describe it out loud, they could
only use the words they had possessed at the time it happened.

113
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:09,620
The experience was locked inside the language they had back
then — and back then, they had almost none.

114
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:12,440
So your babyhood isn't only deleted.

115
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:15,900
Part of it was filed in a
format you can no longer open.

116
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:23,100
If infantile amnesia were purely a matter of brain hardware, it
should be identical for every human being on the planet.

117
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:24,420
It isn't.

118
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:27,020
And that is the biggest clue of all.

119
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:37,860
When researchers in New Zealand compared earliest memories across cultures, they found that Māori adults
reported first memories from noticeably earlier in childhood than their neighbors of European descent.

120
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:40,780
Some from before the age
of two and a half.

121
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:42,360
The brains are the same.

122
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:48,760
What's different is the culture — a strong tradition
of talking about the past, out loud, in detail.

123
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:51,340
The same effect shows up inside families.

124
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:56,510
Parents differ enormously in how they talk to
small children about things that already happened.

125
00:06:56,510 --> 00:06:59,410
Some ask questions and
build the story together.

126
00:06:59,410 --> 00:07:01,120
Remember the ducks?

127
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:02,350
What color were they?

128
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:03,560
Who got scared?

129
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:05,970
Others just confirm it and move on.

130
00:07:05,970 --> 00:07:10,720
The children of the first kind end
up with earlier, richer, more durable memories.

131
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:11,760
Consistently.

132
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,910
Which means the boundary of your own memory
is not purely a fact about your brain.

133
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:19,450
It is partly a fact about how much
somebody talked to you when you were three.

134
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:23,380
Memory, it turns out, is something
you learn how to do.

135
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:25,630
And somebody has to teach you.

136
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:31,620
Quick historical detour, because the first person to
name this problem had a very different theory.

137
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:36,380
Sigmund Freud noticed infantile amnesia
over a century ago.

138
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:37,920
His explanation?

139
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:44,140
You do remember your early childhood — but it was so
full of forbidden feelings that your mind buried the entire thing.

140
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:45,620
On purpose.

141
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:47,550
Very dramatic, Sigmund.

142
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:49,540
There's essentially no evidence for that.

143
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:56,020
But to be fair to the man with the cigar, he
spotted something real that everybody else had walked straight past.

144
00:07:56,020 --> 00:07:59,160
The memories are not
missing because nothing happened.

145
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:02,000
Something is actively keeping
them from you.

146
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,380
He just got the something wrong.

147
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:06,020
It isn't shame.

148
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:07,470
It's construction work.

149
00:08:07,470 --> 00:08:11,530
Now for the part of this
video that might genuinely bother you.

150
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:14,890
That earliest memory you came up
with a few minutes ago?

151
00:08:14,890 --> 00:08:17,180
There's a decent chance it never happened.

152
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:31,480
In a study of over six thousand people, researchers found that nearly forty percent reported a first memory from age
two or younger — an age at which, as we have just established, lasting episodic memories basically cannot survive.

153
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,480
Those weren't recordings.

154
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,550
They were reconstructions.

155
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:49,140
Assembled later out of family stories, photographs, home videos and imagination, and then quietly filed
under "things I actually experienced." The person remembering has no way to tell the difference.

156
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:54,990
It feels exactly like a real memory — because as
far as your brain is concerned, it is one.

157
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:58,120
Your brain does not play
memories back like video.

158
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:01,130
It rebuilds them from
scratch, every single time.

159
00:09:01,130 --> 00:09:07,270
And it will cheerfully use whatever materials it finds lying around
— including a photograph you saw when you were nine.

160
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:10,270
And then there's the
cruelest detail of all.

161
00:09:10,270 --> 00:09:12,690
Small children do remember being babies.

162
00:09:12,690 --> 00:09:18,720
Ask a three-year-old about something that happened when they were eighteen
months old, and sometimes they will tell you about it.

163
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:20,330
The memories are in there.

164
00:09:20,330 --> 00:09:21,830
For a while.

165
00:09:21,830 --> 00:09:25,760
But around the age of seven,
those early memories start dissolving.

166
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:27,290
Fast.

167
00:09:27,290 --> 00:09:40,640
Which means that at some point in your childhood, you were the last person alive holding the last
surviving memory of your own babyhood...
...you remembered it one final time...
...and then you never thought about it again.

168
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,030
And you didn't even notice it happen.

169
00:09:43,030 --> 00:09:44,670
So those missing years.

170
00:09:44,670 --> 00:09:46,420
Are they just gone?

171
00:09:46,420 --> 00:09:48,080
Not exactly.

172
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:52,700
Because your brain has more than one memory
system, and only one of them got wiped.

173
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:54,410
The events were deleted.

174
00:09:54,410 --> 00:09:56,020
The skills stayed.

175
00:09:56,020 --> 00:10:01,170
The language you are listening to right
now — installed during the missing years.

176
00:10:01,170 --> 00:10:02,710
The way you walk.

177
00:10:02,710 --> 00:10:04,250
The way you laugh.

178
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:08,830
Your gut sense of whether the world
is basically safe or basically dangerous.

179
00:10:08,830 --> 00:10:12,090
Whether you expect the people
who leave to come back.

180
00:10:12,090 --> 00:10:16,300
All of it was written during
the years you cannot access.

181
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:22,740
Not as memories, but as the operating system you are
running on right now, today, while you watch this.

182
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:24,960
You can't remember being that baby.

183
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:26,850
But you did not lose them.

184
00:10:26,850 --> 00:10:29,640
Everything they learned, you
are still using.

185
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:31,270
Every single day.

186
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:33,650
You're just running on
somebody else's notes.

187
00:10:33,650 --> 00:10:40,920
And if you're now wondering whether the rest of your
memories are trustworthy...
...that is a question for another video.
